'''2.''' Relating or pertaining to woe; expressing woe; characterized by sorrow or woe; deplorable.
{{smaller block|She . . . sings extemporally a ''woeful'' ditty.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 836.}}}}
{{smaller block|A Trumpet shall sound from Heaven in ''woful'' and terrible Manner.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, iv. 43.}}}}
{{smaller block|He [Lord Ranelagh] died hard, as their term of art is here, to express the ''woful'' state of men who discover no religion at their death.
{{right|''Swift.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O, ''woeful'' day! O, day of woe to me!</poem>
{{right|''A. Philips'', Pastorals, iv.}}}}

'''3.''' Wretched; paltry; mean; pitiful.
{{smaller block|<poem>What ''woful'' stuff this madrigal would be!</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Criticism, l. 418.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 2.''' Mournful, calamitous, disastrous, afflictive, miserable, grievous. See ''woe''.}}

'''woefully, wofully''' (wō′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' In a woeful manner.
{{smaller block|Which now among you, who lament so ''wofully'', . . . has suffered as he suffered? ''V. Knox'', Works, VI., serm. v.}}
{{smaller block|It is a fact of which many seem ''wofully'' ignorant.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 484.}}}}

'''woefulness, wofulness''' (wō′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wofulnesse''; < ''woeful'' + ''-ness''.] The state or quality of being woeful; misery; calamity.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thys day can noght be saad the heuinesse mad,
Noght halfe the ''wofulnesse'' the cite hauing.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 648.}}}}
{{smaller block|The lamenting Elegiack . . . surely is to be praysed, either for compassionate accompanying iust causes of lamentation, or for rightly paynting out how weake be the passions of ''wofulnesse''.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie, p. 44.}}}}

